From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Crunch41 <70764418+Crunch41@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 04:01:20 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Harden lobby registration and JWT verification

A public room talks to the lobby web service; these things there could
break registration, hang the room's shutdown or flood its log:

- Every lobby request went out as POST since upstream moved to
  Hydrocarbon (83fde0738), whatever the caller asked for: the room
  fetched the JWT public key with a POST and "unregistered" with a POST
  to /lobby/<id>, so it stayed listed after stopping. Requests now use
  their own method (hydro::perform).
- RoomJson::Register read the lobby's reply without checking it. An
  empty, non-JSON or incomplete reply (a proxy error page, an outage)
  registered the room with an empty id. Empty replies, parse errors and
  a missing "id" now return a WrongContent WebResult, which the session
  reports like any other announce error.
- The announce thread itself is wrapped in a try/catch, so an exception
  is logged instead of calling std::terminate. The thread still ends:
  the room keeps running, unannounced.
- GetPublicKey fills a static public key cache without a lock. Its only
  caller today is the VerifyUserJWT constructor, on the main thread
  before the room opens, so nothing races yet; a mutex guards the cache
  so that stays true if another caller appears.
- RoomJson::Delete unregistered the room on a thread that used the
  RoomJson's own client and room_id, kept in a jthread member that was
  destroyed after them, and the room crashed on the way out (a segfault
  in memmove; upstream since c2e74a938, July 2026). The web client also
  has no read timeout, so a slow lobby could hold the room past docker
  stop's 10 seconds. The DELETE now runs on its own thread with copies of
  what it needs (3 seconds to connect), and the destructor waits for it
  up to 5 seconds; the room unregisters if the lobby answers in time and
  stops either way.
- Every player without a lobby token logged "Verification failed" at
  Info level. Only that exact case (cpp-jwt's "decode"/2,
  SignatureFormatError, from an empty token) is silenced; expired tokens
  and bad signatures are still logged.

Tests: tests/test_room_protocol.py runs a public room against a stand-in
lobby, stops it while the lobby is still answering the DELETE, and
requires a clean exit and a DELETE request, also within 10 seconds when
the lobby takes 25 seconds to answer; it also checks that joining
without a token still works. Registration replies and JWT verification
against a real lobby are not covered.
